1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,080
And now, to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:11,060
You know why they didn't make two Yogi Bears?

3
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:14,960
Well, they tried, but somebody made a boo-boo.

4
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:17,320
Oh, man.

5
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:20,960
Hey, remember what Yogi said.

6
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:22,960
It ain't over till it's over.

7
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:25,160
Oh, wait a minute.

8
00:00:26,220 --> 00:00:27,960
That phone hasn't run in a while.

9
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:30,280
That's the clue phone.

10
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:32,540
Let me get that.

11
00:00:34,940 --> 00:00:35,660
What?

12
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:40,340
Oh.

13
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:42,480
Hey, okay, thanks.

14
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:50,640
That was Yogi Bear, not Yogi Bear, who said,

15
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:52,420
it ain't over till it's over.

16
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:01,660
Okay, so now it's really over.

17
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:04,000
Hey, this is podcast number 49.

18
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:15,440
Hey, hey!

19
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:41,880
Well, welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

20
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:42,979
This is Clark Green.

21
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:49,980
Over on iTunes, got this review this week.

22
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:53,820
If you subscribe on iTunes, you can leave a review or a rating.

23
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:56,980
So feel free to do that.

24
00:01:57,260 --> 00:01:59,840
But from Skater Brian, he said,

25
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:02,860
I'm a new Assistant Scoutmaster and did scouting myself as a boy.

26
00:02:03,060 --> 00:02:07,860
I've been searching the internet for any help or resources to help me guide our new troop.

27
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:10,460
And this podcast nails it perfectly.

28
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:15,800
Tons of great information and from what I've seen, the perspective is dead on.

29
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:18,900
I'm looking forward to learning more from the site and further podcasts.

30
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:20,840
Thank you for your work and for sharing it with us.

31
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:27,400
Well, thank you, Brian, for getting in touch and for leaving that review over on iTunes.

32
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:33,960
By email this week from Jeff, who is the Scoutmaster of Troop 237.

33
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:36,980
He says, thanks for the podcast and the jokes.

34
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:38,460
I really do like them.

35
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:39,800
Hmm.

36
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:43,120
I guess he means both the podcast and the jokes.

37
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:46,560
Yeah, another minority view there, Jeff.

38
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:47,320
But that's okay.

39
00:02:47,620 --> 00:02:48,240
That's all right.

40
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:50,060
I kind of like the jokes, too.

41
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:57,560
The podcast helped me remember why I got involved with Scouts as an adult, even when things get tough, as they sometimes do.

42
00:02:57,680 --> 00:02:58,300
Best wishes?

43
00:02:58,720 --> 00:02:59,320
By email.

44
00:02:59,580 --> 00:03:00,420
Tracy wrote in.

45
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:02,740
By email from Tracy.

46
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:08,840
Dear Clark, thanks once again this week and every week for an informative and inspirational podcast.

47
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:17,300
Just wanted to post a brief comment to tell you how much we appreciate your insight and also the great comments from other Scouters across the country.

48
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:20,100
Well, thank you very much, Tracy, for your kind words.

49
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:26,800
And I read that one as a reminder to everyone that this doesn't need to be a monologue.

50
00:03:27,560 --> 00:03:32,240
The Scoutmaster podcast can be a bit of a dialogue, a little bit of a discussion.

51
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:34,080
So do get in touch.

52
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:40,600
And the easiest way to do that is to email me at clarkgreen at gmail.com.

53
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:46,980
And that's C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N at gmail.com.

54
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:54,940
I heard again this week from Larry Geiger, who liked Green Grow the Rushes in the last podcast.

55
00:03:57,120 --> 00:04:01,600
Rather than that Boy Scout rappy thing that we had, I guess, in podcast number 47.

56
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:04,360
He said, that's something I can understand and enjoy.

57
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:06,300
Thank you, Mr. Scoutmaster, sir.

58
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:13,740
And Larry is responsible, by the way, for the Yogi Bear joke at the beginning of this week's podcast.

59
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:19,380
I don't have Larry's email address handy.

60
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:22,400
But you can send comments about that joke to me.

61
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:24,680
I'll see that Larry gets them.

62
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:34,820
While back on the blog, I posted a YouTube video that somebody had gone to the trouble of recording

63
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:43,320
from an old BSA film strip that was used for adult leader training 30-some years ago.

64
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:54,380
And I remember seeing that film strip as part of, I guess, what at the time was called Scoutmaster Basic or something like that.

65
00:04:54,560 --> 00:05:00,060
I remembered the film strip because it was such a great expression of the patrol system,

66
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:07,400
the patrol method, empowering youth leaders and the relationship between the Scoutmaster and his youth leadership.

67
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:10,080
Brian commented on that post.

68
00:05:10,179 --> 00:05:10,520
He said,

69
00:05:11,520 --> 00:05:13,419
Very helpful and informative.

70
00:05:13,659 --> 00:05:16,719
The BSA has been teaching boys to become men for 100 years,

71
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:20,799
and the method to the teaching and the movement are built on timeless principles.

72
00:05:21,539 --> 00:05:26,440
And this film strip was every bit as useful to me today as it was to Scoutmasters 30 years ago.

73
00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:27,919
Thank you for posting it.

74
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:30,940
Anyway, it's honestly a big hole in our troop right now.

75
00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:37,580
And anyone has any advice for teaching a group of boys who have little to no experience with scouting

76
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:40,140
how to lead their own patrols and troop,

77
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:44,120
Brian, I think what you do is you just keep at it.

78
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:45,420
You would be surprised.

79
00:05:45,780 --> 00:05:51,080
Even a group of boys who has little to no experience with scouting will pick this up right away.

80
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:55,580
So what I've done is I've taken the audio part of that video,

81
00:05:56,380 --> 00:05:58,380
and I've cleaned it up a little bit.

82
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:04,760
It still sounds as though you're listening to it in a tin can somewhere,

83
00:06:05,140 --> 00:06:09,960
but some of you are well under the age of 50,

84
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:12,120
and you might not know what a film strip is.

85
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:13,799
So let me tell you what it was.

86
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:18,020
A film strip was kind of like a 35mm roll of film,

87
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:23,460
and almost like slides, if you've ever seen slides.

88
00:06:24,979 --> 00:06:26,580
Maybe you don't know what slides are.

89
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:27,060
Jeez.

90
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:32,039
But anyway, there was a projector,

91
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:34,979
and it had a little crank on it,

92
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:40,940
and what you would do is you would advance the film strip frame by frame,

93
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:43,799
and it wasn't animated or anything.

94
00:06:43,799 --> 00:06:47,440
It was just, you know, kind of like a PowerPoint presentation.

95
00:06:48,020 --> 00:06:54,820
And the audio section was usually on a, like a, um, an LP, a vinyl LP.

96
00:06:55,140 --> 00:06:56,940
There was a little boop signal.

97
00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:57,580
Boop!

98
00:06:57,799 --> 00:06:59,520
To change the frame.

99
00:07:00,580 --> 00:07:06,479
So when I did the audio, uh, portion of the YouTube video of the film strip,

100
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:08,039
do I need to draw a diagram?

101
00:07:09,260 --> 00:07:10,719
It gets a little complex.

102
00:07:11,180 --> 00:07:13,400
But, uh, I took out all the boops,

103
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:16,020
and I tried to improve the sound as best I could.

104
00:07:16,020 --> 00:07:17,880
The reason I went to the trouble to do this

105
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:23,099
is because I think that this is probably one of the more quintessential,

106
00:07:23,419 --> 00:07:28,299
more effective pieces of training on the subject of youth leadership

107
00:07:28,299 --> 00:07:31,060
and the relationship of adults to youth leadership

108
00:07:31,060 --> 00:07:32,799
and the way the patrol system works,

109
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:35,719
um, as any that's ever been produced.

110
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:39,320
And so I thought it would be a good idea

111
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:42,260
to have it here on the Scoutmaster Podcast

112
00:07:42,260 --> 00:07:45,980
for you to listen to and to share with your fellow leaders.

113
00:07:46,320 --> 00:07:46,640
Please.

114
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:48,600
So without, uh, further ado,

115
00:07:49,020 --> 00:07:50,460
let's get started, shall we?

116
00:07:50,460 --> 00:08:15,960
It was a warm Saturday in May.

117
00:08:15,960 --> 00:08:18,400
Western District was having its camparee.

118
00:08:18,659 --> 00:08:20,260
From Truth 28's campsite

119
00:08:20,260 --> 00:08:22,940
came the sounds of vigorous physical activity

120
00:08:22,940 --> 00:08:26,299
and the joyous shouts of men and boys working and playing together.

121
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:28,240
Come on, you guys.

122
00:08:28,299 --> 00:08:29,760
The inspectors will be here any minute.

123
00:08:30,280 --> 00:08:31,859
Anderson, tighten up those ropes.

124
00:08:32,919 --> 00:08:34,360
Colucci, you wash this pot.

125
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:34,919
Yes, sir.

126
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:36,079
Well, wash it again.

127
00:08:36,159 --> 00:08:37,760
We're not having a fire sale this morning.

128
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:40,559
Where's the water for the macaroni, Schultz?

129
00:08:40,799 --> 00:08:42,559
Don't you think those inspectors might wonder

130
00:08:42,559 --> 00:08:44,260
how come it's noon and nobody's cooking?

131
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:45,840
Relax, buddy.

132
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:47,300
It'll come out all right.

133
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:47,720
What?

134
00:08:48,380 --> 00:08:49,500
Oh, hi, Ben.

135
00:08:49,740 --> 00:08:51,880
Come on over to my place to relax a minute.

136
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:52,640
Relax?

137
00:08:52,840 --> 00:08:53,460
Are you kidding?

138
00:08:53,740 --> 00:08:55,220
We're going to be inspected any minute.

139
00:08:55,640 --> 00:08:56,800
Thanks, but I can't come out.

140
00:08:56,980 --> 00:08:58,800
The troop looks fine, Rod.

141
00:08:59,000 --> 00:08:59,400
Really.

142
00:08:59,920 --> 00:09:00,700
Come on over.

143
00:09:01,060 --> 00:09:02,400
You don't have to be here every minute.

144
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:03,420
Come on.

145
00:09:03,780 --> 00:09:04,880
Oh, uh, okay.

146
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:08,760
Gee, your troop looks great, Ben.

147
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:10,900
What time do the inspectors hit you?

148
00:09:11,100 --> 00:09:12,260
Oh, they haven't been here yet.

149
00:09:12,620 --> 00:09:12,939
What?

150
00:09:13,180 --> 00:09:14,340
And you're just taking it easy?

151
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:17,180
I guess that's the difference between you and me, Rod.

152
00:09:17,700 --> 00:09:18,300
What's that?

153
00:09:18,460 --> 00:09:21,660
Well, you act as if that's you about to get inspected over there

154
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:22,680
instead of a scout troop.

155
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:25,080
Well, gosh, it is me in a way.

156
00:09:25,660 --> 00:09:26,880
I want my boys to win,

157
00:09:26,939 --> 00:09:29,240
and I certainly want to do everything I can to help them.

158
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:31,300
Don't you think I want my boys to win, too?

159
00:09:31,720 --> 00:09:32,520
Well, sure you do.

160
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:36,880
But you don't see me out there whipping them in a shape, do you, buddy?

161
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:38,980
Well, you don't have to, Ben.

162
00:09:39,140 --> 00:09:40,140
Your boys are trained.

163
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:41,040
My kids are...

164
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:43,780
Your kids are just as well trained as mine are, Rod.

165
00:09:43,980 --> 00:09:44,640
Maybe better.

166
00:09:45,380 --> 00:09:47,260
You just don't trust them, that's all.

167
00:09:47,620 --> 00:09:47,960
What?

168
00:09:48,380 --> 00:09:49,600
Think about it a minute.

169
00:09:50,360 --> 00:09:52,380
Look at the way you have them set up camp.

170
00:09:53,100 --> 00:09:54,360
Do you always set up that way?

171
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:55,360
Sure we do.

172
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:56,360
Works fine.

173
00:09:56,860 --> 00:09:59,819
But how can your patrols work if they don't camp by themselves?

174
00:09:59,820 --> 00:10:02,760
Don't they automatically look to you for leadership?

175
00:10:03,040 --> 00:10:04,480
Oh, come on, Ben.

176
00:10:04,820 --> 00:10:07,020
And I'll bet you planned the menus for this weekend.

177
00:10:07,360 --> 00:10:07,680
Right?

178
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:08,640
Well, sure.

179
00:10:09,380 --> 00:10:10,400
Where'd you get the menus?

180
00:10:10,860 --> 00:10:12,160
Scoutmaster's handbook, mostly.

181
00:10:12,360 --> 00:10:12,800
Right.

182
00:10:13,100 --> 00:10:15,580
And you're the only guy in the troop who can read?

183
00:10:16,300 --> 00:10:17,640
Well, I...

184
00:10:17,640 --> 00:10:20,760
Just now, I saw you chewing out a boy about a dirty pot.

185
00:10:21,300 --> 00:10:22,600
Doesn't that boy have a patrol leader?

186
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:23,240
Well, sure.

187
00:10:23,340 --> 00:10:25,620
And that pot's a perfect example of his leadership.

188
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:26,960
Is that it?

189
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:30,400
Or is it that you don't really expect him to lead?

190
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:32,880
Or want him to?

191
00:10:33,180 --> 00:10:33,439
What?

192
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:34,540
Come on, Rod.

193
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:35,860
I want to show you something.

194
00:10:36,180 --> 00:10:38,880
Well, you're not making much sense, but you make a good cup.

195
00:10:39,020 --> 00:10:40,600
See that patrol over there?

196
00:10:40,920 --> 00:10:41,960
Holy smoke, Ben.

197
00:10:41,980 --> 00:10:42,980
There's the inspectors.

198
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:43,440
Sure.

199
00:10:43,960 --> 00:10:44,720
Aren't you going over?

200
00:10:45,020 --> 00:10:45,500
Why?

201
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:47,900
They can find me if they want me.

202
00:10:48,380 --> 00:10:53,280
The point I'm trying to make is that I haven't spoken to anybody in that patrol since last night

203
00:10:53,280 --> 00:10:54,760
except to say good morning.

204
00:10:55,620 --> 00:10:57,840
They're doing this inspection on their own.

205
00:10:58,520 --> 00:11:01,080
After all I know, they're foaming on their faces.

206
00:11:01,460 --> 00:11:02,560
Don't you care?

207
00:11:02,940 --> 00:11:03,860
About inspection?

208
00:11:04,380 --> 00:11:04,780
No.

209
00:11:04,780 --> 00:11:09,040
If they want to win, they know what they have to do.

210
00:11:09,660 --> 00:11:12,140
If they need help, they know where to find me.

211
00:11:12,740 --> 00:11:17,079
What I do care about is whether they learn to do things like this on their own.

212
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:19,040
I'm not a babysitter.

213
00:11:19,640 --> 00:11:21,540
Hey, what's with the calisthenics?

214
00:11:21,680 --> 00:11:22,920
You guys on a fitness kick?

215
00:11:23,520 --> 00:11:24,680
Nothing out of the ordinary.

216
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:26,319
Just this patrol.

217
00:11:26,860 --> 00:11:28,079
And they're going hot and heavy.

218
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:31,040
Something the patrol leader dreamed up with help from Charlie.

219
00:11:31,780 --> 00:11:32,819
He's going out for track.

220
00:11:32,820 --> 00:11:36,080
And you act like you don't really have much to do.

221
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:38,700
For instance, what did you do this morning around here?

222
00:11:38,900 --> 00:11:39,980
Well, let's see.

223
00:11:40,120 --> 00:11:44,480
I got up, shaved, took the raccoon's invitation to breakfast.

224
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:46,760
They had some questions about skill awards.

225
00:11:47,340 --> 00:11:51,280
Then I had a session with my senior patrol leader so he'd know what has to happen today.

226
00:11:51,980 --> 00:11:57,460
Then two guys in the Raven Patrol had a big fight about whether the eggs should be scrambled or hard-boiled.

227
00:11:57,940 --> 00:12:00,540
And I spent about 20 minutes getting them back in shape.

228
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:03,380
You mean the patrol leader couldn't handle it?

229
00:12:03,699 --> 00:12:04,380
Oh, right.

230
00:12:04,459 --> 00:12:05,300
I get the needle.

231
00:12:06,099 --> 00:12:10,939
You know, I think the hardest thing about the patrol method is knowing when to step in.

232
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:13,800
I thought that was a time to step in.

233
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:14,660
And I did.

234
00:12:15,199 --> 00:12:16,120
I hope I did right.

235
00:12:16,500 --> 00:12:16,880
Let's see.

236
00:12:17,540 --> 00:12:22,099
Then I spent about an hour with a meek little tenderfoot showing him how to use a compass.

237
00:12:22,100 --> 00:12:24,660
You teach skills like that?

238
00:12:24,660 --> 00:12:28,160
Actually, I don't do much skill teaching except the boy leaders.

239
00:12:28,380 --> 00:12:33,160
But this little kid has no father at home, and I try to spend some time with him when I can.

240
00:12:33,380 --> 00:12:35,780
Gee, I don't have time for things like that.

241
00:12:36,120 --> 00:12:37,060
I know you don't.

242
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:39,740
You're too busy being the great leader to everybody.

243
00:12:40,260 --> 00:12:42,520
Boy, are you giving me a hard time today.

244
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:46,260
If it wasn't for your hospitality, I'd go sit in somebody else's tent.

245
00:12:46,260 --> 00:12:47,780
No, you wouldn't.

246
00:12:48,100 --> 00:12:52,620
If I hadn't dragged you away from your sight, you'd still be over there beating those kids into the ground.

247
00:12:53,560 --> 00:12:56,120
Let me read you what Bacon Powell says.

248
00:12:56,700 --> 00:12:59,140
You know, he's the fellow who started this business.

249
00:13:00,160 --> 00:13:09,360
The object of the patrol system is mainly to give real responsibility to as many of the boys as possible with a view to developing their character.

250
00:13:09,760 --> 00:13:11,200
Huh. Makes sense.

251
00:13:11,200 --> 00:13:18,520
The patrol system leads each boy to see that he has some individual responsibility for the good of his patrol.

252
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:27,080
The main object is not so much saving the scoutmaster trouble as to give responsibility to the boy.

253
00:13:27,760 --> 00:13:33,220
To get first-class results, you have to give the boy leaders real free-handed responsibility.

254
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:39,440
If you only get partial responsibility, you will only get partial results.

255
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:42,840
Hmm. That's me, friend. Partial results.

256
00:13:43,540 --> 00:13:44,960
Huh. I guess you're right.

257
00:13:45,740 --> 00:13:48,660
Hey, it's late. I gotta get back to my trooper.

258
00:13:48,860 --> 00:13:52,020
Rod, do your kids a favor when you get back, huh?

259
00:13:52,560 --> 00:13:53,440
What's that, teacher?

260
00:13:54,060 --> 00:13:55,660
Go sit in your tent.

261
00:14:06,240 --> 00:14:07,180
What's this, fellas?

262
00:14:07,180 --> 00:14:09,760
We're making a tripod for our pots and pans.

263
00:14:10,120 --> 00:14:12,719
Oh, good idea. Nice going, guys.

264
00:14:13,479 --> 00:14:15,439
Jerry, could I see you a moment?

265
00:14:15,920 --> 00:14:16,719
Okay, Mr. Gibson.

266
00:14:17,859 --> 00:14:21,140
Well, Mr. Senior Patrol Leader, how did we do an inspection?

267
00:14:21,780 --> 00:14:24,959
I guess we did okay, but they didn't like the way we have the tents lined up.

268
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:26,500
They said it wasn't by patrol.

269
00:14:26,959 --> 00:14:29,699
Hmm. Guess we'd better have a patrol leader's meeting.

270
00:14:29,700 --> 00:14:36,420
Fellas, would you rather put your tents up by patrol the way they do it in the other troops?

271
00:14:37,220 --> 00:14:37,820
Sure, yeah.

272
00:14:37,820 --> 00:14:38,380
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

273
00:14:38,440 --> 00:14:39,420
Okay, yes, sir.

274
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:41,020
All right, now, now let's see.

275
00:14:41,780 --> 00:14:45,120
Now you fellas work it out with Jerry and rearrange them if you want to.

276
00:14:45,320 --> 00:14:45,880
All right.

277
00:14:46,120 --> 00:14:47,340
Yeah, great.

278
00:14:48,400 --> 00:14:52,200
Jerry, have the patrol leaders report to you when they're all cleaned up from lunch.

279
00:14:52,700 --> 00:14:54,020
Then you check them out.

280
00:14:54,620 --> 00:14:55,580
Aren't you going to?

281
00:14:55,580 --> 00:14:59,180
No, I, well, I have a very important meeting.

282
00:15:01,060 --> 00:15:08,400
Yes, it was at the Spring Campery when Scoutmaster Rod Gibson first began to understand what is meant by the patrol method.

283
00:15:09,100 --> 00:15:18,340
Slowly but surely, wherever he was, even at work, when he thought about the troop, he found himself thinking how he might make the patrol method work better.

284
00:15:19,060 --> 00:15:25,100
Spring became summer, and summer drifted into fall, and winter came in its turn.

285
00:15:25,100 --> 00:15:30,080
And when spring came around again, there was a familiar sight out on a certain field.

286
00:15:30,840 --> 00:15:33,460
Scoutmaster Palmer, you're working too hard.

287
00:15:33,720 --> 00:15:36,320
Oh, hello, Rod. How are you doing?

288
00:15:36,840 --> 00:15:38,700
Fine. Got time to chat?

289
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:40,720
With you, anytime. Let's go.

290
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:43,340
Here, Ben. Sit in my chair.

291
00:15:43,680 --> 00:15:46,240
Your chair? You use this?

292
00:15:46,440 --> 00:15:47,440
You bet I do.

293
00:15:47,440 --> 00:15:52,160
You know, Ben, I had planned to resign right after last year's Camperini.

294
00:15:52,560 --> 00:15:53,620
I didn't know that.

295
00:15:53,820 --> 00:15:58,300
Sure. I didn't feel I was getting anywhere with the troop. It had become a real grind.

296
00:15:58,920 --> 00:16:04,280
Then you came along and showed me the way you operate, and talked me into trying it, and I did.

297
00:16:05,140 --> 00:16:09,800
Well, I hate to admit it, but you were right. I never had really trusted my boys.

298
00:16:09,800 --> 00:16:16,339
Ben, I never had any idea what these kids could do until I really gave them a chance to.

299
00:16:16,839 --> 00:16:19,920
And here I was the guy who had been holding them back all that time.

300
00:16:20,959 --> 00:16:24,099
It's hard work, this patrol method, but it sure pays off.

301
00:16:24,420 --> 00:16:26,959
Gee, I'm sure glad this has worked out for you, Rod.

302
00:16:27,560 --> 00:16:33,740
But now that you've got the patrol method under control, there are a few other things you want to learn.

303
00:16:33,740 --> 00:16:35,740
For instance, did you know...

304
00:16:35,740 --> 00:16:54,560
Now it's noon and nobody's cooking.

305
00:16:55,400 --> 00:16:57,880
Relax, buddy. It'll come out all right.

306
00:16:58,060 --> 00:16:58,260
What?

307
00:16:58,260 --> 00:17:08,379
Come on over to my place to relax a minute.

308
00:17:08,740 --> 00:17:10,160
Relax? Are you kidding?

309
00:17:10,480 --> 00:17:11,300
We're going to be in...

310
00:17:11,300 --> 00:17:12,379
Oh, hi, Ben.

311
00:17:12,700 --> 00:17:13,060
Come on.

312
00:17:13,180 --> 00:17:13,859
Hi, Ben.

313
00:17:14,160 --> 00:17:14,560
Come on.

314
00:17:14,680 --> 00:17:15,359
Hi, Ben.

315
00:17:15,640 --> 00:17:16,040
Come on.

316
00:17:16,280 --> 00:17:16,800
Hi, Ben.

317
00:17:16,859 --> 00:17:17,819
Expect it any minute.

318
00:17:18,280 --> 00:17:19,420
Thanks, but I can't come out.

319
00:17:19,839 --> 00:17:21,680
The troop looks fine, Rod.

320
00:17:21,879 --> 00:17:22,260
Really?

321
00:17:23,160 --> 00:17:24,220
Expect it any minute.

322
00:17:24,680 --> 00:17:25,680
Expect it any minute.

323
00:17:25,819 --> 00:17:26,819
So there you have it.

324
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:27,720
That's the film strip.

325
00:17:28,260 --> 00:17:31,320
It's a little dated in parts, you know?

326
00:17:31,720 --> 00:17:36,600
And it's a little cheesy in a delightfully kind of filmstrippy way.

327
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:39,240
But it still has the right message.

328
00:17:39,379 --> 00:17:41,200
It has exactly what we're looking for.

329
00:17:41,580 --> 00:17:43,340
And I encourage you to share it.

330
00:17:43,640 --> 00:17:51,640
There'll be a link on the blog post that contains this podcast so that you can find the film strip in YouTube videos.

331
00:17:53,379 --> 00:17:55,740
And it should be part of everybody's training.

332
00:17:56,120 --> 00:17:56,980
It just really should.

333
00:17:56,980 --> 00:18:03,120
Well, I want to thank you for listening to this Scoutmaster Podcast number 49.

334
00:18:04,460 --> 00:18:11,040
You can read the Scoutmaster blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

335
00:18:11,880 --> 00:18:19,220
You can follow us at Scoutmaster Blog on Facebook and ScoutmasterCG on Twitter.

336
00:18:19,220 --> 00:18:32,440
You can subscribe to the Scoutmaster Podcast on iTunes.

337
00:18:32,440 --> 00:18:36,740
And when you do, feel free to leave a comment or a review or a rating.

338
00:18:36,740 --> 00:18:47,400
The easiest way to get in touch is to email me, Clark Green, with your comments and questions at ClarkGreen at gmail.com.

339
00:18:47,400 --> 00:18:53,160
That's C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-E-N at gmail.com.

340
00:18:53,160 --> 00:19:12,920
The Scoutmaster Blog and the Scoutmaster Podcast are not official publications of the Boy Scouts of America.

341
00:19:13,380 --> 00:19:16,540
Nor are they endorsed or sponsored by the Boy Scouts of America.

342
00:19:16,540 --> 00:19:16,860
Nope.

343
00:19:17,659 --> 00:19:18,520
Hey, it's just me.

344
00:19:19,180 --> 00:19:19,520
You know?

345
00:19:19,899 --> 00:19:21,080
Talking into a microphone.

346
00:19:22,159 --> 00:19:25,879
Trying to lend a hand to Scout leaders and maybe have a little bit of fun along the way.

347
00:19:25,880 --> 00:19:36,060
So how does a bear stop a VCR?

348
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:39,420
You just press the pause button.

349
00:19:39,420 --> 00:20:02,300
Well, before we leave you, let's make sure to hear from the founder of Scouting, Sir Robert Baden-Powell.

350
00:20:02,820 --> 00:20:03,260
Sir Robert?

351
00:20:04,040 --> 00:20:06,380
Good luck to you and good camping.

352
00:20:07,740 --> 00:20:09,200
Why, thank you, Sir Robert.

353
00:20:09,420 --> 00:20:11,120
Until next time.

